# docs/roadmap/roadmap.snapshot.json is a generated read-replica of the
# daemon's roadmap that has to stay committed (CI can't reach a local
# daemon's SQLite to regenerate it, and tests/purser/test-roadmap-snapshot.js
# + docs/audits/tenancy-boundary.spec.json both treat the committed copy as
# an oracle, not a cache). It conflicts on almost every merge/rebase because
# every regeneration stamps a fresh generatedAt and two branches routinely
# add different new items to the same array.
#
# The `roadmap-snapshot` merge driver (lib/roadmap-snapshot-merge.ts via
# scripts/merge-roadmap-snapshot.ts) resolves it per-item by slug instead of
# by line, so disjoint additions merge cleanly and only a genuine same-slug
# edit on both sides surfaces as a real conflict.
#
# The driver is registered per-checkout by `npm install` (package.json
# "prepare" -> scripts/setup-git-merge-driver.mjs), since `git config
# merge.*.driver` does not travel with a clone. A checkout that never ran
# `npm install` gets ordinary textual conflicts on this file — same as
# before — not a merge failure. Run it manually with:
#   node scripts/setup-git-merge-driver.mjs
# Note this only covers local `git merge`/`git rebase`; GitHub's own
# "Update branch" / merge-queue merges run server-side and never consult it.
docs/roadmap/roadmap.snapshot.json merge=roadmap-snapshot
